{:nav-title "Predicates"
 :content
 [:div
  [:h1 "Filtering with Predicates"]
  [:p "So far we matched patterns by specifying exact values like "
   [:code "\"Bulbasaur\""] ". Predicates let us filter rows using arbitrary truthy expressions."]
  [:p "A predicate clause is a function call wrapped in an extra pair of brackets:"]
  [:ui/code "[(> ?speed 100)]"]
  [:p "Every variable used in the predicate must be bound somewhere in the query, not necessarily by an earlier clause. "
   "A variable bound nowhere is the real error:"]
  [:ui/code "?nope not bound in expression clause: [(> ?nope 100)]"]
  [:p "Order still matters for two other reasons, though: data patterns run in the order written, so put the "
   "selective ones first for performance, and predicates read best right after the clause that binds their variables. "
   "Let's find all Pokemon with a speed stat above 100:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name ?speed
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [?e :stat/speed ?speed]
    [(> ?speed 100)]]]
  [:ui/try-tip
   [:p "Move " [:code "[(> ?speed 100)]"] " to the top of the " [:code ":where"] " clauses, above the patterns "
    "that bind " [:code "?e"] " and " [:code "?speed"] ". The query still returns the same "
    [:ui/value "pokemon" [:find (count ?e) . :where [?e :stat/speed ?speed] [(> ?speed 100)]]]
    " Pokemon."]]
  [:p "We can compare two bound variables against each other. "
   "Here are the Pokemon whose defense is strictly higher than their attack:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name ?attack ?defense
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [?e :stat/attack ?attack]
    [?e :stat/defense ?defense]
    [(> ?defense ?attack)]]]
  [:p [:code "not="] " is a predicate too: " [:code "[(not= ?a ?b)]"] " keeps a row only if the two values differ. "
   "The next chapter puts it to use."]
  [:p "Predicates work on strings too, this site allows "
   [:code "clojure.string/starts-with?"] " and " [:code "clojure.string/includes?"]
   ". In a real Datomic app, any pure " [:code "clojure.core"] " function is available (except "
   [:code "eval"] "), along with any Java or Clojure function on the classpath of the process "
   "that runs the query. With the Client API that process is the server, not your application, "
   "so your own functions aren't available unless they are deployed there too. "
   "Here we find Pokemon whose name starts with \"S\":"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [(clojure.string/starts-with? ?name "S")]]]
  [:p [:strong "Good to know: "] [:code "="] ", " [:code "!="] ", " [:code "<"] ", " [:code "<="] ", "
   [:code ">"] " and " [:code ">="] " are "
   [:a {:href "https://docs.datomic.com/query/query-data-reference.html#range-predicates"} "range predicates"]
   ". Datomic answers them with an index instead of checking every value "
   "(on Datomic Pro this only applies to attributes marked " [:code ":db/index true"] " or "
   [:code ":db/unique"] "; Datomic Cloud and Local, which this site runs on, index every attribute already), "
   "so they are much more efficient than the same test written with another function. "
   "Datomic's own "
   [:a {:href "https://docs.datomic.com/query/query-data-reference.html#built-in-functions"} "built-in functions"]
   " are also cheaper than your own, since they run inside the query engine. "
   "Our database is too small to notice, but this matters in a real one. "
   "Strings sort by character code, not dictionary order, so uppercase comes before lowercase. "
   "Every name here is capitalized, which is why the names starting with \"S\" are also the names "
   "from \"S\" up to, but not including, \"T\". That is the same "
   [:ui/value "pokemon" [:find (count ?e) . :where [?e :pokemon/name ?name] [(clojure.string/starts-with? ?name "S")]]]
   " Pokemon as before:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [(>= ?name "S")]
    [(< ?name "T")]]]
  [:p "Or find Pokemon whose name contains \"saur\":"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [(clojure.string/includes? ?name "saur")]]]
  [:ui/try-tip
   [:p "Combine two predicates to find Pokemon whose name starts with \"S\" "
    [:em "and"] " whose speed is above 100. "
    "Or find Pokemon whose attack " [:em "and"] " defense are both above 80."]]

  [:div {:class "recap"}
   [:span {:class "recap-label"} "You can now"]
   [:ul
    [:li "Write a predicate clause to filter rows on any truthy expression, "
     "including a comparison between two bound variables."]
    [:li "Recognize the \"not bound in expression clause\" error, "
     "and know every predicate variable must be bound somewhere in the query."]
    [:li "Know that Datomic answers range predicates with an index, "
     "and that strings sort by character code, not dictionary order."]]]]}
